## Critical Rules

### Never Invent Information
Only extract what exists in source files. If something is unclear, incomplete, or missing, flag it explicitly:
- `GAP: [description of what's missing]` — knowledge that should exist but doesn't
- `CONFLICT: [description]` — contradictions between files
- `Assumption: [what was assumed and why it might be wrong]`

### {{VOICE_RULE_SECTION}}

### Overview Sections Must Lead With the Claim
The first sentence of every Overview must state what the concept IS or DOES. Not who said it. Not why it matters philosophically. The bot retrieves the first chunk of each page — make it the answer, not the preamble.

### Cross-Domain Connections Are First-Class Content
When extracting knowledge, ALWAYS note cross-domain connections. These appear as callout blocks:
```
> **Cross-domain:** [How this concept connects to the owner's specific situation, numbers, and context]
```

### Bidirectional Linking Is Mandatory
If page A references or depends on page B, page B must link back to A. Run the lint pass to catch drift.

### {{ANONYMIZATION_RULE_SECTION}}

### Never Merge Distinct Concepts
If a source treats two things as separate, they get separate pages. Don't collapse them even if they seem related — the distinctions are intentional.

### Empty Stubs Must Not Be Ingested
A stub page (boilerplate header, no real content) is worse than a broken wikilink for RAG retrieval — the system ingests it as if it contains knowledge. Delete stubs before running the Phase 6 ingest sync. High-traffic stubs (referenced by 3+ pages) must be filled with real content, not deleted.

### Frontmatter Must Use YAML Format
Always fence frontmatter with `---`. Never use Obsidian Dataview `::` syntax for `confidence:`, `source-date:`, or any other field. The consuming app's parser expects YAML — a format mismatch silently defaults every page to the lowest-confidence label, corrupting all bot caveating behavior.

